---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { getProjectById } from '../../../data/projects';

const project = getProjectById('disaster-recovery');

if (!project) {
  throw new Error('Backup & Recovery project data could not be found.');
}
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Backup & Recovery Platform"
  description="A resilience platform built around automated protection, recovery assurance, monitoring visibility and operational evidence."
>

<section class="hero">
  <div class="container hero-grid">

    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Infrastructure Resilience</p>

      <h1>
        Recovery confidence built through
        <span>automation, validation and evidence.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="hero-summary">
        A practical resilience platform that transforms backups from a passive
        safety measure into an operational capability. Protection, recovery
        assurance, monitoring visibility and controlled processes provide
        evidence that services can be restored when required.
      </p>

    </div>


    <aside class="scope-card">

      <p class="eyebrow">Scope</p>

      <div class="scope-item">
        <span>Protection</span>
        <strong>Automated backup workflows</strong>
      </div>

      <div class="scope-item">
        <span>Assurance</span>
        <strong>Recovery validation and integrity checks</strong>
      </div>

      <div class="scope-item">
        <span>Visibility</span>
        <strong>Prometheus and Grafana monitoring</strong>
      </div>

      <div class="scope-item">
        <span>Operations</span>
        <strong>Reporting and controlled recovery processes</strong>
      </div>

    </aside>

  </div>
</section>


<section class="section">

<div class="container">

<p class="eyebrow">The Challenge</p>

<h2>
Backups existed. Recovery confidence needed to be demonstrated.
</h2>

<div class="prose">

<p>
A successful backup job does not prove that an environment can recover from
failure.
</p>

<p>
The challenge was creating a resilience capability that provided visibility
across backup status, repository health, recovery readiness and operational
state across a multi-host infrastructure environment. DietPi backup,
archive and retention automation is now captured as non-secret Git-owned
source, with credentials and live repository state kept separately protected.
A non-destructive recovery rehearsal reconstructed 38 managed files and 14
executables beneath an isolated root, then passed hash, mode, syntax, systemd,
manifest-integrity and secret-exclusion checks without changing live services.
</p>

</div>

</div>

</section>


<section class="section section-soft">

<div class="container">

<p class="eyebrow">Platform Architecture</p>

<h2>
From protection to operational confidence.
</h2>


<div class="architecture">

<div>
<h3>Backup Automation</h3>
<p>
Repeatable backup processes provide consistent recovery points.
</p>
</div>

<div>
<h3>Validation</h3>
<p>
Integrity checks and recovery assurance provide evidence rather than assumption.
</p>
</div>

<div>
<h3>Monitoring</h3>
<p>
Metrics expose resilience health through the existing observability platform.
</p>
</div>

<div>
<h3>Reporting</h3>
<p>
Technical state is converted into operational information.
</p>
</div>

</div>

</div>

</section>


<section class="section">

<div class="container">

<p class="eyebrow">Capabilities</p>

<h2>
Building resilience through visibility and assurance.
</h2>


<div class="cards">

<article>
<h3>Protection</h3>
<p>
Automated backup workflows reduce dependency on manual processes and create
repeatable recovery points.
</p>
</article>


<article>
<h3>Recovery Assurance</h3>
<p>
Recovery readiness is measured rather than assumed, improving confidence
when services or data need to be restored.
</p>
</article>


<article>
<h3>Operational Visibility</h3>
<p>
Backup and resilience information is exposed alongside wider infrastructure
monitoring through dashboards and metrics.
</p>
</article>


<article>
<h3>Management Reporting</h3>
<p>
Operational information is presented clearly so technical health can support
decision making.
</p>
</article>

</div>

</div>

</section>


<section class="section section-soft">

<div class="container">

<p class="eyebrow">Operational Recovery Example</p>

<h2>
Recovery principles applied beyond data protection.
</h2>

<div class="prose">

<p>
The same resilience principles apply when recovering services, not only data.
</p>

<p>
A controlled production deployment process demonstrated this approach through:
</p>

<ul>
<li>maintenance mode activation</li>
<li>change-controlled deployment</li>
<li>validation checks</li>
<li>failure detection</li>
<li>service restoration</li>
</ul>

<p>
Recovery is not simply restoring files. It is restoring capability.
</p>

</div>

</div>

</section>


<section class="section">

<div class="container">

<p class="eyebrow">Engineering Outcome</p>

<h2>
Turning backups into resilience.
</h2>

<div class="prose">

<p>
The outcome is not just a backup system.
</p>

<p>
It is a resilience capability built around protection, validation, visibility
and recovery confidence.
</p>

</div>

</div>

</section>


</BaseLayout>


<style>

.hero {
  padding: 6rem 0 4rem;
}

.hero-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1.4fr .6fr;
  gap: 4rem;
  align-items: start;
}


h1 {
  font-size: clamp(3.5rem, 7vw, 6rem);
  line-height: .95;
  letter-spacing: -.055em;
}

h1 span {
  display:block;
  color:var(--primary);
}


.hero-summary {
  max-width:900px;
  font-size:1.15rem;
  color:var(--text-muted);
}


.scope-card {
  padding:2rem;
  border:1px solid var(--border);
  border-radius:var(--radius);
  background:var(--surface);
}


.scope-item {
  padding:1.2rem 0;
  border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);
}


.scope-item:last-child {
 border-bottom:0;
}


.scope-item span {
 display:block;
 color:var(--text-muted);
 margin-bottom:.4rem;
}


.section {
 padding:5rem 0;
}


h2 {
 max-width:900px;
 font-size:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.2rem);
 line-height:1.05;
}


.prose {
 max-width:850px;
 color:var(--text-muted);
 font-size:1.1rem;
}


.architecture,
.cards {
 display:grid;
 grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));
 gap:1.5rem;
 margin-top:2rem;
}


.architecture div,
.cards article {
 padding:1.8rem;
 border:1px solid var(--border);
 border-radius:var(--radius);
 background:var(--surface);
}


@media(max-width:900px){

.hero-grid,
.architecture,
.cards {
 grid-template-columns:1fr;
}

}

</style>
